# `expr -> T` was the arrow cast; it is removed, and stage0 refuses every VALUE-position
# occurrence at parse time. stage1 read the arrow as an ordinary binary operator, so the
# sentence never appeared and the BACKEND declined the whole function instead -- the user
# was told "backend emitted no functions for this unit", not which construct was the problem.
def widen(value: i64) -> i64:
    narrowed: i64 = value -> u8
    return narrowed
